---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/attentive-vs-mailchimp';
const title = 'Attentive vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Attentive email and SMS with Mailchimp audience CRM, ecommerce workflows and eligible paid SMS for Shopify drops, consent and migration.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Attentive fit?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Attentive when a Shopify team wants to operate email and SMS together around subscriber capture and shopping events. Confirm email enablement, purchased scope and event mapping before designing journeys."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does Mailchimp fit?",
    "answer": "Mailchimp can fit a team already using its audience CRM, email and ecommerce workflows. SMS requires an eligible paid marketing plan, supported market, approved application and purchased credits; confirm account eligibility before scheduling texts."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does email permission authorize SMS?",
    "answer": "No. Keep channel, purpose, capture source, timestamp and disclosure evidence separate. An order notification permission does not establish promotional permission. Review requirements for each destination with qualified advice."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should we add Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Only if a documented email workflow gap justifies another system. Both candidates offer email. Compare the extra integration, identity and suppression work before adding an email layer."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should we compare cost?",
    "answer": "Request account-specific quotes for email, SMS, contacts, credits, overages, carrier charges, services and exit terms. Compare net contribution after returns, cost of goods, fulfillment and campaign costs on equivalent eligible cohorts."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should migration work?",
    "answer": "Name the merchant account owner, export consent and suppression evidence, map identities, pause overlapping sends and reconcile imported records before activation. Test purchase exits and opt-outs in the destination; keep uncertain records suppressed."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14">
    <nav><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Attentive vs Mailchimp</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Attentive vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl">Choose around who operates your Shopify email, SMS and customer records. Attentive supports email and SMS; Mailchimp combines audience CRM and email with SMS for eligible paid accounts after approval. Neither choice replaces merchant control of consent, inventory and campaign collisions.</p>
    </header>
    <TldrBox title="Conditional fits" picks={[
      { label: 'Attentive', tool: 'Attentive', reason: 'Evaluate coordinated email and SMS around Shopify shopping events; confirm enabled products and data flow.' },
      { label: 'Mailchimp', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Evaluate continuity with existing audience CRM and ecommerce workflows; verify paid SMS eligibility, approval and credits.' },
    ]} />
    <article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>What the account must support</h2>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Attentive</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Channels</td><td>Confirm email and SMS configuration in the proposed agreement.</td><td>Email plus SMS add-on for eligible paid accounts in supported markets; approval and credits required.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shopify data</td><td>Validate capture and product-view, cart and purchase events against actual profiles.</td><td>Validate connected store data, audience membership and ecommerce workflow triggers.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Operations</td><td>Assign approvals, response handling and export access.</td><td>Assign audience ownership, permissions and SMS registration responsibility.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Check current <a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/360041854231-Shopify">Attentive Shopify documentation</a>, <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/about-sms-marketing/">Mailchimp SMS eligibility</a> and <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/set-up-sms-marketing/">SMS setup requirements</a>. Features and billing must be confirmed for your account, market and agreement.</p>
      <h2>Proposed merchant workflow tests</h2>
      <p>The named scenarios below are planning examples retained from this comparison, not verified customer deployments or measured results. Run them only after the operator approves the eligible audience, purpose, inventory and stop conditions.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Patchwork Denim: send one-of-one listing drops to style-preference segments. Compare with a broader eligible cohort while preventing sold-item links and duplicate profile sends.</li>
        <li>Loom &amp; Frame: disclose edition size and test a sell-through reminder to repeat collectors rather than assuming scarcity beats a launch message. Check remaining stock at send time.</li>
        <li>Driftwood Surf Co: coordinate seasonal boardshort drops with swell-forecast email content; test waitlist restock targeting against a broader eligible announcement.</li>
        <li>Noir Collective: plan a Thursday evening release with VIP early access. Test lower broadcast frequency against the existing schedule using comparable cohorts and recipient-local restrictions.</li>
        <li>SoleDrop ATX: preserve size-specific waitlist priority, send an early access link without requiring a discount code, and release general email only after an operator checks inventory.</li>
        <li>Festival Threads Co: map city-specific tour dates to venue and recipient timezones. Set an explicit per-city message cap and cancel reminders when routing changes.</li>
        <li>Vinyl Vault Records: disclose pressing quantity and distinguish waitlist, repeat collectors and high-value buyers. Validate value calculations and eligibility before granting priority.</li>
        <li>Bloom &amp; Wick: plan holiday teasers and shipping deadlines ahead of the season. Separate gift-bundle SMS from self-purchase scent education email and check promise consistency.</li>
        <li>Peak Trail Gear: isolate bargain-sensitive clearance cohorts from full-price loyalists. Use an eligible holdout to assess whether discounts add contribution rather than merely shifting purchases.</li>
        <li>Sauce Lab: tie batch-number scarcity messages to verified remaining bottles. Set launch and reminder caps, cancel on sellout and test whether the reminder adds value.</li>
        <li>Ember Ceramics: match a small kiln release to waitlist priority and fulfillment capacity. Coordinate maker-story email with a restrained VIP text; do not promise stock to the whole queue.</li>
        <li>Ridge Run Coffee: exclude active subscribers from non-subscriber flash offers. Test delayed subscription and purchase events so queued messages stop before dispatch.</li>
        <li>Cipher Audio: coordinate a teaser email with launch SMS to eligible engaged browsers. Compare a no-discount launch with an approved incentive treatment using net contribution.</li>
        <li>Arena Energy: target the relevant gym-bag segment for an athlete flavor collaboration. Keep a free-shaker email offer distinct from timing-only SMS and check landing-page eligibility.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Approval, identity and suppression</h2>
      <p>Give one merchant operator authority over the shared calendar, inventory release, offers and final send approval. Validate email and phone identities against Shopify customer and order IDs, including guest checkout, changed numbers and duplicate profiles. A contact merge must not turn an email subscriber into an SMS subscriber.</p>
      <p>Store channel and purpose permission, source, timestamp and disclosure version. Separate transactional notices from promotional drops. Define opt-out scope from the request and applicable requirements; do not silently restore permission during imports. Keep uncertain records suppressed and review quiet hours, timezone uncertainty and daylight-saving behavior for the destinations you serve.</p>
      <p>In each candidate account, test opt-out, purchase, subscription activation, sold-out stock and a queued message arriving out of order. Check both channels, record event-to-exit delay and reconcile source events with final send logs. A connected integration alone does not prove a pending message was cancelled. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent basics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> when setting operator policy.</p>
      <h2>Economics and test design</h2>
      <p>Request written scope and pricing for email, SMS credits or usage, contacts, overages, carrier fees, services and migration. Message encoding and destination affect billable segments; count actual segments rather than assuming every text is one charge. Model peak-season volume and list growth using each quote.</p>
      <p>Predefine equivalent eligible cohorts, a holdout, attribution rules and a duration based on purchase cycles and adequate observations. No fixed pilot length proves a winner. Compare net sales after discounts and returns, less cost of goods, fulfillment, message charges, allocated platform fees and incremental support. Do not subtract discounts twice from already-discounted net sales. Compare contribution per eligible customer, including holdout customers, rather than treating attributed revenue as incremental lift.</p>
      <h2>Ownership and exit reconciliation</h2>
      <p>The merchant should control account administration, billing, sender access and export permissions. Before moving, verify contract notice, sender transfer conditions and what consent evidence, channel statuses, suppression history and workflow data can be exported. Pause overlapping automations, map source IDs and compare imported, rejected, duplicate and suppressed counts with the source. Reconcile sample records and final sends before retiring the old account; keep a rollback plan without reactivating opted-out records.</p>
      <h2>Conditional decision</h2>
      <p>Attentive may fit a team organizing Shopify email and SMS around shopping events when its enabled products and operating controls satisfy these tests. Mailchimp may fit a team keeping audience CRM, email and ecommerce workflows together when its paid SMS account is eligible and approved. Select the account that meets the merchant workflow and measured economics. Add Sequenzy or another email tool only for a defined gap that outweighs the added identity and suppression work.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Attentive vs Mailchimp FAQ" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
